Signed-off-by: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>

Here is the root cause:
The bug arises from the incorrect assumption that if two VMAs share the same `->anon_vma`, the list of anon_vmas attached to those VMAs will be identical. This assumption fails due to the optimization that tracks the `degree` of anon_vmas, leading to scenarios where an anon_vma can be reused for one VMA while still being associated with another VMA. This results in potential memory management issues, such as merging pages into a VMA that is not correctly linked to the corresponding anon_vma, causing dangling pointers during reverse mapping walks.

